order: 22
---
short_label: XI.22
---
red_highlight: yes
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If there are three plane angles such that the sum of any two is greater than the remaining one, and they are contained by equal straight lines, then it is possible to construct a triangle out of the straight lines joining the ends of the equal straight lines.
----
proof:

Let there be three plane angles {ABC|angB}, {DEF|angE}, and {GHK|angH}, of which the sum of any two is greater than the remaining one, so that the sum of the angles {ABC|angB} and {DEF|angE} is greater than the angle {GHK|angH}, the sum of the angles {DEF|angE} and {GHK|angH} is greater than the angle {ABC|angB}, and, further, the sum of the angles {GHK|angH} and {ABC|angB} is greater than the angle {DEF|angE}. Also let the straight lines {AB}, {BC}, {DE}, {EF}, {GH}, and {HK} be equal.

Join {AC}, {DF}, and {GK}.

I say that it is possible to construct a triangle out of straight lines equal to {AC}, {DF}, and {GK}, that is, that the sum of any two of the straight lines {AC}, {DF}, and {GK} is greater than the remaining one.

<center>
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="550" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="XI.22 diagram" src="propXI22.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's figure: the three plane angles ABC, DEF, GHK as filled
    // triangles on equal sides (C, D, F, G, K slide on circles of radius
    // AB), and the angle KHL copied from ABC at H with HL cut off equal,
    // KL and GL joined.
    //
    // Joyce draws every line here as a triangle edge except GL. The walk
    // needs the six equal sides before AC, DF, GK are joined, and HL
    // before KL, so AB, BC, DE, EF, GH, HK, AC, DF, GK, HL, KL are solid
    // slide twins, hidden at rest (initiallyHidden), stroked from the end
    // they are drawn from. ABC and HKL trace and fill at the I.4 step,
    // DEF at the I.24 step, GHK at the close. No triangle is highlighted,
    // so no faceless twins are needed. Triangle and angle names collide
    // (ABC, DEF, GHK): the triangles own them, the markers angB, angE,
    // angH take the display override. Equal angles share a colour (angB,
    // angKHL blue). The three markers at H lie side by side (GHK, KHL)
    // and around both (GHL), so they are pinned to opt out of ring
